 # Otis Urethrotome
 
-**Blind (non-visual) internal-urethrotomy instrument** that combines **calibrated dilation** with a **controlled longitudinal cold-knife incision** at the **12 o'clock dorsal midline** as the device is withdrawn. The historical predecessor to direct-vision internal urethrotomy (DVIU); now largely supplanted by the **Sachse direct-vision urethrotome** but retaining a niche role in meatal / fossa-navicularis stricture and in resource-limited settings. Named for **Fessenden Nott Otis (1825–1900)**, the American urologist who is also the namesake of the [Otis Bougie-à-Boule](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/otis-bougie). The **ICS male LUTS-surgery terminology document** explicitly defines blind urethrotomy as "opening of the stricture with the use of a special instrument (Otis urethrotome) to perform the incision without direct visualization."<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+The **Otis urethrotome** is a reusable, non-optical instrument for blind internal urethrotomy. International Continence Society terminology defines blind urethrotomy as opening a urethral stricture with a special instrument—specifically giving the Otis urethrotome as the example—without direct visualization.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
 
-## Design
+It is historically important, but it is not interchangeable with direct-vision internal urethrotomy (DVIU). Contemporary stricture selection and outcomes should be taken from the clinical [DVIU and Urethral Dilation](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu) page rather than extrapolated from DVIU to this blind device.
 
-- **Long slender metal shaft** introduced per urethra with the blade retracted and the expandable segment collapsed.
-- **Calibrated expandable distal segment** — turning a screw on the handle widens the segment to a precise French diameter shown on a graduated handle scale, allowing **measured dilation** before the cut.
-- **Concealed retractable cold-knife blade** housed within the shaft; when deployed, the blade extends from a longitudinal slot and cuts at the **12 o'clock position** as the instrument is withdrawn.
-- **Handle** carries the expansion screw, blade-deployment mechanism, and the French-gauge scale.
-- **Stainless steel**, fully autoclavable.
+## Current device configurations
 
-The two functions in one instrument — **dilate-then-incise on withdrawal** — distinguish the Otis from both a plain sound (no cut) and the Sachse / Collins-knife systems (cut under vision, no calibrated dilation).
+Current catalogs show that there is no single universal Otis configuration:
 
-## Reconstructive-Urology Uses
+- Richard Wolf lists a reusable Otis urethrotome with straight, curved, and spherical exchangeable tips, a 15–45 Fr range, 236 mm length, and a 12-o'clock cutting position.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
+- FDA AccessGUDID lists a V. Mueller/Steris straight Otis urethrotome supplied with three guide tips and two blades, 30 cm overall length, as commercially distributed.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
+- AccessGUDID also lists an RZ Medizintechnik set with two knives and three atraumatic tips in commercial distribution.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
 
-### Stricture incision (historical and niche)
+These are device-record facts, not a procedural prescription. Tip selection, target caliber, blade use, reprocessing, and compatible accessories must follow the instructions for the exact instrument.
 
-- **Meatal and fossa-navicularis strictures** — the short straight distal urethra is the safest anatomy for a blind dorsal-midline cut; the Otis retains a small role here when DVIU equipment is not at hand.
-- **Resource-limited settings** — when an endoscopic urethrotome and irrigation system are unavailable.
-- **Bulbar strictures** — historically managed with the Otis; modern practice uses [DVIU](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu) instead.
+## Operating concept
 
-### Prophylactic urethrotomy before TURP (historical)
+The instrument is passed with the knife protected, mechanically expands or calibrates the urethra, and makes a longitudinal incision as the blade is drawn through the narrowed segment. Because the cut is not observed endoscopically, true-lumen access and tissue depth cannot be confirmed in the same way as with an optical urethrotome.
 
-The Otis was widely used as a **preliminary cut at the meatus and distal urethra before TURP** to accommodate the 24–28 Fr resectoscope sheath and reduce post-TURP stricture rates.
+That limitation matters: forcing the instrument across resistance or assuming that a fixed target caliber is safe can create bleeding, false passage, or injury beyond the intended scar. The reviewed sources do not support one universal anesthesia method, adult target diameter, catheter size, or postoperative dwell time for every Otis device and stricture.
 
-- **Schultz 1989 RCT (n = 185)** — Otis urethrotomy before TURP **4% stricture** vs catheter dilation **16%** (significant).<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
-- **Nielsen 1989 RCT (n = 200)** — **no significant difference**: urethrotomy + TURP 14% vs TURP alone 21%; the authors concluded that "urethrotomy before TURP cannot prevent postoperative urethral strictures."<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
-- The practice has **largely fallen out of favor** with smaller-caliber endoscopic platforms (HoLEP, ThuLEP, bipolar enucleation) that do not require a 28-Fr sheath.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>
+## Evidence in context
 
-For procedural context, see [DVIU and Urethral Dilation](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu).
+Historical series described use of the Otis method for urethral stricture. A 1983 consecutive series reported 82% success (95% confidence limits 71%–90%) among 72 patients at mean 29-month follow-up, but the accessible record is an abstract and predates contemporary stricture classification and outcome standards.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
 
-## Technique — Otis Method
+Before TURP, prophylactic Otis urethrotomy produced conflicting results:
 
-1. **General or regional anesthesia** — the blind dilation component is more painful than a visual urethrotomy under local.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
-2. **Insertion** with blade retracted and segment collapsed.
-3. **Advancement past the stricture** — if too tight, preliminary [filiform-and-follower](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/filiforms-followers) or sound dilation.
-4. **Expansion** — turn the screw to widen the segment to the target caliber (~ **28–30 Fr** in the adult male).
-5. **Blade deployment and slow withdrawal** — the blade makes a clean dorsal-midline longitudinal cut as the expanded segment is pulled back through the stricture.
-6. **Foley catheter 18–22 Fr** placed at the end of the case.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
+- a randomized 185-patient trial reported postoperative stricture in 4% after Otis urethrotomy versus 16% after two-day catheter dilation;<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
+- a separate randomized 200-patient trial found 14% after internal urethrotomy plus TURP versus 21% after TURP alone at three months, a nonsignificant difference, and concluded that urethrotomy did not prevent postoperative stricture.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>
 
-## Otis vs Sachse (DVIU)
+These older TURP studies do not establish routine prophylactic urethrotomy for current endoscopic prostate platforms. They should be read as historical procedure-specific evidence, not as a general indication for an Otis urethrotome.
 
-| Feature | **Otis (blind)** | **Sachse (DVIU)** |
-|---|---|---|
-| **Visualization** | None — blind | **Direct endoscopic vision** |
-| **Mechanism** | Expand → blade cuts on withdrawal | Cold-knife incision under vision |
-| **Incision control** | Cuts at 12 o'clock through whatever tissue is there | **Surgeon selects site / depth** under vision |
-| **Stricture traversal** | Must pass instrument through / past stricture first | Can incise progressively from proximal to distal |
-| **Anesthesia** | Generally GA / regional | Often **local** in select cases |
-| **Era of predominance** | Late 1800s – 1970s | **1973 – present** |
-| **False-passage risk** | Higher (blind) | Lower (visual) |
-| **Calibrated dilation built in** | **Yes** | No |
-
-## Outcomes
-
-- **Frohmüller / Bülow 1975** — Otis urethrotomy in 23 patients; **~ 60% required no further dilations**; low complication rate.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Hjortrup 1983** — 72 consecutive patients over 5 years; **success 82%** (95% CI 71–90%) at mean 29-month follow-up.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
-- **Cochrane (Wong 2012)** — the only RCT directly comparing dilation to urethrotomy used the **Sachse** technique, not the Otis; head-to-head data between Otis and Sachse remain sparse.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
-- **AUA 2023** — dilation and DVIU are interchangeable first-line options for short bulbar strictures, with the highest success rates in bulbar strictures &lt; 1 cm; success falls rapidly with length, recurrence, penile location, and ischemic etiology.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup>
-- **Verla 2019 review** — DVIU success up to **~ 80% for primary strictures &lt; 2 cm**; figures likely transfer roughly to Otis urethrotomy in equivalent anatomy.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
-
-## Safety Profile
-
-- **False passage** — the principal complication of the blind technique; the rationale for the modern shift to DVIU.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
-- **Uncontrolled incision depth** — the 12 o'clock cut goes through whatever tissue is at the blade; in pendulous urethra this risks corporal-tunica injury.
-- **Urethral hemorrhage, UTI, bacteremia** — standard urethral-instrumentation risks.
-- **Stricture recurrence** — the underlying biology (re-fibrosis) is not changed by switching from blind to visual incision; recurrence remains the principal limitation of any endoscopic stricture management.
-
-## Limitations and Modern Positioning
+## Otis and optical urethrotomy
 
-The Otis has been **supplanted by the Sachse DVIU** for the great majority of indications because:<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
-
-- **Direct vision** spares healthy mucosa and limits false-passage risk.
-- **Progressive incision** from proximal to distal eliminates the need to traverse the stricture first.
-- **Office / local-anesthesia DVIU** is possible in selected cases.
-- The Otis remains useful for **meatal / fossa-navicularis** strictures and in **equipment-limited** settings.
-
-## Historical Significance
-
-The Otis urethrotome was one of the first instruments to move stricture management **beyond simple dilation toward controlled incision**. Otis's principle — that a **measured, calibrated longitudinal cut** heals across to a wider lumen rather than tearing scar — is the conceptual ancestor of modern DVIU and remains the foundation of endoscopic stricture management today.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
+| Feature | Otis urethrotome | Optical/Sachse urethrotome |
+|---|---|---|
+| View of incision | None | Endoscopic view |
+| Mechanical calibration/expansion | Built into common Otis designs | Not the defining mechanism |
+| Tip and blade configuration | Varies by manufacturer | Varies by optical system |
+| Evidence transfer | Otis-specific evidence required | DVIU evidence applies to the clinical procedure, not automatically to Otis |
 
-See also: [Otis Bougie-à-Boule](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/otis-bougie), [Van Buren Sound](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/van-buren), [Filiforms & Followers](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/filiforms-followers), [Balloon Dilator](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/balloon-dilator), [S-Shaped Coaxial Dilators](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/s-dilators), [DVIU and Urethral Dilation](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu).
+See also: [Sachse Urethrotome](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/sachse-urethrotome), [Otis Bougie-à-Boule](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/otis-bougie), [DVIU and Urethral Dilation](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu).
 
